---
title: Integrate your first app
description: Register a client, run the Authorization Code + PKCE flow, and log a user in.
section: Getting started
---

This is the chapter that pays off the rest. By the end, a real application performs a real login
against your tenant.

## Register the application

Go to **OAuth Clients → Create**, and fill in the two fields that actually matter:

**Client type.** Can this application keep a secret?

- **Public** — a SPA, a mobile app, anything running on the user's device. It cannot hold a secret
  (anyone can read the bundle), so PKCE is the *only* proof it offers at the token endpoint.
- **Confidential** — a server-side web app. It gets a `client_secret` and authenticates with it.

If you are unsure, you are almost certainly building a confidential client — see the note on
backends below.

**Redirect URIs.** Where GateKeeper is allowed to send the user back after they authorize.

> [!WARNING]
> Redirect URIs are matched **exactly**. No wildcards, no prefix matching, no ignoring the trailing
> slash. `https://app.example.com/callback` and `https://app.example.com/callback/` are two
> different URIs. This is the single most common integration failure, and it is deliberate — loose
> redirect matching is how tokens get stolen.

Save, then copy the **client id** from the Credentials tab. If the client is confidential, copy the
secret too:

> [!CAUTION]
> The client secret is shown **once**, at creation and after each rotation. It is stored only as an
> Argon2 hash, so GateKeeper genuinely cannot show it to you again. If you lose it, rotate it — and
> expect the old one to stop working immediately.

## The flow

GateKeeper implements **OAuth 2.1**: Authorization Code with **mandatory PKCE**. There is no
implicit flow and no password grant — both are removed in OAuth 2.1, and neither can be re-enabled.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant U as User's browser
    participant App as Your app
    participant GK as GateKeeper

    App->>App: generate code_verifier + code_challenge (S256)
    App->>U: redirect to /oauth/authorize
    U->>GK: GET /oauth/authorize?client_id&redirect_uri&code_challenge
    GK->>U: hosted login page
    U->>GK: credentials (+ MFA if required)
    GK->>U: redirect back with ?code=...
    U->>App: GET /callback?code=...
    App->>GK: POST /oauth/token (code + code_verifier)
    GK->>GK: verify challenge matches verifier
    GK-->>App: access_token, id_token, refresh_token
```

Two details to hold on to:

- **PKCE** is what stops a stolen authorization code from being redeemed by anyone else. Your app
  invents a random `code_verifier`, sends only its SHA-256 hash up front, and reveals the verifier
  when redeeming the code. An attacker who intercepts the code never saw the verifier.
- The authorization code is **single-use and short-lived** (60 seconds). Redeem it immediately.

## Exchange the code

```bash
curl -X POST https://gatekeeper.example.com/oauth/token \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "grant_type": "authorization_code",
    "code": "the-code-from-the-redirect",
    "code_verifier": "the-verifier-you-generated",
    "client_id": "your-client-id",
    "redirect_uri": "https://app.example.com/callback"
  }'
```

You get back an access token, an ID token, and — if you asked for the `offline_access` scope — a
refresh token.

## Where to put the tokens

This is the decision that determines whether your integration is secure, and it is worth being
blunt about: **do not store tokens in `localStorage`.** Any script on your page can read them.

The pattern to copy is a **backend-for-frontend (BFF)**: your server performs the code exchange,
keeps the tokens server-side, and hands the browser nothing but an encrypted, `httpOnly` session
cookie. The browser never sees an access token, so a cross-site scripting bug cannot steal one.

> [!TIP]
> There is a complete, working reference implementation of exactly this in the repository, under
> `frontends/sveltekit-bff/` — a SvelteKit BFF that does the server-side exchange and stores the tokens in an
> AES-256-GCM encrypted `httpOnly` cookie. Read it before writing your own.

## Refresh tokens

Refresh tokens **rotate**: each use returns a new one and invalidates the old.

If GateKeeper ever sees an already-used refresh token presented again, it treats that as a stolen
token and **revokes the entire family** — every descendant of that token, immediately. The
legitimate user gets logged out, which is the correct outcome when a token has demonstrably leaked.
The event lands in the audit log as `token.reuse_detected`.

The practical consequence: never use a refresh token twice, and never let two processes share one.

## Verifying tokens in your API

Fetch the tenant's public keys from its JWKS endpoint and verify the signature. Your JWT library
does this for you — point it at discovery and let it fetch and cache the keys by `kid`.

```http
GET /.well-known/jwks.json
```

Because each tenant signs with its own key, verification *is* your tenant isolation. A token from
another tenant fails the signature check; you do not have to remember to compare a `tenant_id`
claim yourself.
